WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.920
hi i'm james murphy. have you ever come across&nbsp;
an error when trying to import a module? in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.520
this case it's telling me that i can't import my&nbsp;
function a from a partially initialized module a.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:19.200
scrolling over it tells me that the most likely&nbsp;
cause is a circular import. in this video we'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.160
go over how these circular imports commonly&nbsp;
arise and what are the main ways to fix them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.840
here's the simplest and most common way&nbsp;
that this kind of import cycle can happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.360
at runtime main tried to import something&nbsp;
from a, so it starts initializing a. it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:40.880
starts running the code from a, but the first&nbsp;
line in a is to import something from module b,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:47.600
so it stops initializing a because b has to be&nbsp;
initialized first, and then it hops over to b&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:53.120
and starts running that code instead. but then the&nbsp;
first line in b is that it needs something from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:59.360
a. so it stops running b and goes over to a and&nbsp;
it would like to just start initializing a. but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:04.560
it realizes that a is already in the process of&nbsp;
initialization. so that's where the error occurs/&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.280
we can actually see this chain of events&nbsp;
unfold in the traceback. here we see in main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:17.120
it tries to import function a from module a, which&nbsp;
triggered the import of function b from module b,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:21.120
which then triggered the import of function a&nbsp;
again from module a, which is then where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:27.280
final error occurred. the way that things are&nbsp;
written right now we have an import time cycle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.800
between the modules a and b, but if you&nbsp;
actually look at the names that we imported,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.760
we don't actually use them at import time. we use&nbsp;
them when this function a or when this function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:44.160
b2 are called. since we're not actually calling&nbsp;
these functions at import time, there's no true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:49.520
cyclic dependency, which means we can get rid of&nbsp;
this import cycle. there are three common ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:55.760
to resolve this issue. the first one is to take&nbsp;
your import and put it inside of the function. in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:00.240
python, imports can happen at any time, including&nbsp;
when a function is called, so if this is the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:05.360
place that you need this function b1, then it&nbsp;
could be okay to just do the import right here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.160
this could actually be even more efficient&nbsp;
because if you never call the function a,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.560
then it may be that you never even have to&nbsp;
import b at all. but let's say that you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:18.480
a bunch of different functions in a, and a&nbsp;
lot of different ones use this function b1.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.120
in that case, the better solution would&nbsp;
be to just import the module b directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:30.720
and then in the functions where you use it,&nbsp;
use the longer name module b dot function b1.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:37.760
go ahead and do this conversion for all modules&nbsp;
involved in the cycle. let's see how this resolves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:43.040
the import cycle at runtime. first our main&nbsp;
function tries to import something from a, causing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:48.720
a to start running. in a, we get to the import&nbsp;
module b, which triggers b to start running.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:55.680
in b, when we get to the import module a, because&nbsp;
the a module has already started initializing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:01.120
this module object technically exists. since&nbsp;
it exists, it doesn't start running this again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:05.680
it just says okay that exists so the b module&nbsp;
will just continue on, finish its import,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:09.280
and then after that import is done&nbsp;
the a module will finish importing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:14.720
the third and final common way to get rid of this&nbsp;
import cycle is to just merge the two modules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:20.800
together. no imports, no import cycles. however&nbsp;
if you had two or maybe even more modules in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:25.680
first place, you probably had them for a reason,&nbsp;
so i'm not just going to recommend that you take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:29.520
all of your modules and merge them into one,&nbsp;
that would be kind of silly. so for that reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:34.720
you should probably prefer using one of the first&nbsp;
two methods. let's take a look at another example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:39.120
the next most common kind of import cycle that&nbsp;
you'll run into is due to using type hints.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.120
this example is not like the previous one&nbsp;
because type annotations are defined on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:50.560
function definition or class definition when the&nbsp;
function is defined or when the class is defined,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:56.080
not when it's run. but if you think about it, the&nbsp;
most common use case of type hinting is not even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:02.560
at runtime, it's for static type analyzers like&nbsp;
mypy. if all we care about is static analysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.680
then we don't even need to do the import at&nbsp;
runtime. the typing module provides this variable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:13.120
type checking, which is just false at runtime.&nbsp;
so if you put all the imports that you just need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:18.240
for the purpose of type checking in one of these&nbsp;
if type checking checks, then none of those will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:23.200
actually happen at runtime, completely avoiding&nbsp;
the import loop. but now if you import this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.560
function you're going to get a name error because&nbsp;
this name b hasn't been imported. to fix this, go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:33.680
ahead and add this from future import annotations.&nbsp;
what this actually does is it changes the way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:39.680
annotations work. instead of evaluating b as a&nbsp;
name, all annotations are converted to strings.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:44.960
so now you don't get an error at runtime here&nbsp;
because even though the name b isn't imported,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:49.440
the string b certainly exists. you should&nbsp;
be aware though that whenever you do one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:53.840
of these from future imports it's not&nbsp;
necessarily guaranteed behavior forever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:59.280
right now this causes your annotations to be&nbsp;
handled as strings, but there's actually a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:04.880
competing proposal to make them just be evaluated&nbsp;
lazily so they're just not evaluated at all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:09.760
unless you try to look at them. unless you're&nbsp;
actually inspecting your type hints at runtime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.360
this probably won't make any difference for&nbsp;
your code base, but you should still be aware.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:19.280
an alternative solution is to again just use&nbsp;
import modules instead of from module imports.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:24.880
you will still need the from future import&nbsp;
annotations, and the downside to this approach is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:30.880
that your type hints look kind of, you know, not&nbsp;
as nice. now let's take a look at a very tricky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:36.240
cycle that some larger projects will run into.&nbsp;
this is what i call the sub package init cycle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:41.200
the actual structure of the package is&nbsp;
important for this example so let's go over it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:46.800
first i have a main script, it's outside of the&nbsp;
package and just tries to import some class from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:52.080
some module in the package and do whatever, very&nbsp;
similar to the previous case. now this package,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:58.720
my package, contains two sub packages: sub package&nbsp;
a and sub package b. sub package a contains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:05.280
two modules, module a and c, and sub package&nbsp;
b contains the module b. for whatever reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:10.160
let's assume that module a needs something&nbsp;
out of module b. here's module b, which we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:16.320
also assume depends on module c. but looking at&nbsp;
module c, module c doesn't depend on anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:22.560
so there's no actual cycle here. a depends on b,&nbsp;
and b depends on c. c doesn't depend on anything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:29.360
nevertheless when we try to run it we do get an&nbsp;
import cycle, so what happened? the problem here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:34.880
is not with any of the modules a, b, or c. the&nbsp;
actual issue is in these sub package init files,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:41.760
our subpackage init files here are re-exporting&nbsp;
some names, in this case classes, from some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:47.520
the modules inside of them. doing this is pretty&nbsp;
common in python projects because it allows you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:53.840
have shorter import syntax. here i can just have&nbsp;
a client say from my package dot sub package a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.840 --> 00:07:00.720
import a instead of the longer from my package&nbsp;
sub package a module a import a. the problem with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:06.000
doing this is that every module in a sub package&nbsp;
depends on the init of the sub package, so if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:10.400
init of the sub package depends on all of the&nbsp;
modules in the sub package then you've kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:15.840
made a fake dependency of every module in the sub&nbsp;
package on every other module in the sub package.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:22.800
that means even though c has no imports at all&nbsp;
c now automatically also depends on a because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:28.880
depends on the init. let's see this by starting&nbsp;
in main and following the traceback. main tries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:34.080
to import something from module b, so to import&nbsp;
something from module b we first have to run the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:40.080
sub package init. all sub package b does is import&nbsp;
from module b so we start importing module b.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:45.120
module b wants to import something from&nbsp;
module c, so first we have to initialize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:51.600
sub package a that contains module c. but sub&nbsp;
package a imports from module a, and module a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:57.240
imports from module b, so now we have a cycle&nbsp;
because we haven't finished initializing. the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:03.040
way to get rid of this cycle is to just make all&nbsp;
of your init files blank. if you still want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.040 --> 00:08:07.760
give your users a short way to import names rather&nbsp;
than importing directly from the modules you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:14.320
define what i call an interface package. this is&nbsp;
a package that has no extra modules just its init.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:19.680
inside the init you put all the things that you&nbsp;
wanted to import from your other sub package.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:24.880
now your users can import from the interface&nbsp;
instead of reaching directly into the modules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:29.520
and there's still no import cycles. depending&nbsp;
on your use case you might give the interface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:34.640
a separate name, but a lot of packages just make&nbsp;
the true package that has all the modules in it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:40.640
underscore whatever and then make the interface&nbsp;
name just the whatever. so what was interface a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:44.800
just became sub package a and what was sub&nbsp;
package a became underscore sub package a.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:51.120
finally let's take a look at the worst example.&nbsp;
we're back to just a flat directory structure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:56.880
we just have two modules but in this case both of&nbsp;
the modules depend on each other, a depends on b,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:03.760
b depends on a, and they actually use those&nbsp;
dependencies at import time. this is the worst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:08.720
and most difficult case, primarily because&nbsp;
this often involves an architectural error.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:14.000
if you're actually using something from b&nbsp;
in a, then that thing needs to be defined,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:18.800
so b needs to be initialized before a. but&nbsp;
if you're also using something from a in b,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:24.720
then a needs to be initialized before b. well&nbsp;
which one is it? does b need to come before a,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:29.280
or does a need to come before b? in my&nbsp;
experience, if your modules are that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:34.400
intertwined that they need each other both at&nbsp;
import time, that kind of suggests to me that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:38.720
they should probably be the same module,&nbsp;
merging them together is your best bet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:43.840
however, the most common case that i see&nbsp;
this come up is when someone tries to mix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:50.080
library code with script code. most commonly these&nbsp;
import time dependencies are not really necessary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:54.080
and you should just pull them out into a script&nbsp;
and leave the remaining library code alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:59.280
if you can't strip out these import time&nbsp;
dependencies then i'm sorry, there's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:04.160
much i can do to help you. your application&nbsp;
needs some serious redesigning, it's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:08.960
to be a mess no matter how you do it, and there's&nbsp;
no general advice i can give you. just good luck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:14.320
as always, thank you for watching. thank you to&nbsp;
